                    {
                        "name":  "places/ChIJqxSUlAypQjQRx1e_MFwNIdg/reviews/Ci9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2kxTFQxZEtTMEZZWDIxWVFuUm1NbmMwVldnNE0yYxAB",
                        "relativePublishTimeDescription":  "5 months ago",
                        "rating":  4,
                        "text":  {
                                     "text":  "We came around 1:40 p.m. for lunch and waited about 5–10 minutes. The place was packed with tourists, which made sense given how close it is to the main train station. We ordered four different kinds of shumai along with a wonton soup. The wonton soup was a miss. The classic/original shumai, though, was really good, especially with the chili sauce. While it seemed like the shumai were pre-made or frozen rather than made in-house, they were still quite tasty. Our second favorites were the black truffle shumai and the shrimp dumplings (although the wrapper was too soft and not the right texture). I wouldn’t order the spicy shumai again, as it wasn’t my favorite.",
                                     "languageCode":  "en"
                                 },
                        "originalText":  {
